Seed: 1 | Target day: 20 | Started: 2026-11-10T07:29:04.296Z


The sun is low in the west, and the sea beneath it turns from bronze to iron. The wind is warm and salt, with no rain in it yet. The light is going, but slowly; the evening will be long. This is the last boat. She is old and salt-blanched, and she carries more than she was built to carry: refugees and everything they own, bundles and children and cooking pots lashed down between the benches. The storm took the sail days ago, and the sea kept it. Since then everyone rows in turns, and the oars keep their slow rhythm on the water, hour upon hour, patient beyond the patience of any crew. Everyone is tired. The fresh water is nearly gone; there is only what the rains give, and the rains have been few. The food from home is long gone too: the bread went first, weeks ago, then everything else that could keep, and for a long time now everyone has lived on what the fishing lines bring up, raw fish and rain water, the catch of one day against the hunger of the next. There is no land in sight, in any direction. There is no way back. Among the rowers are your three friends: proud and determined Arik, glum and cynical Bearn, and clever Eya. Near you, your fourth friend Agnar sleeps under a tarp, worn hollow by the voyage. You decide not to wake him. Let the sleeping sleep. Your own turn at the oars is over, and Arik's turn has ended too. He is exhausted, but too proud to show it, and keeps rowing, reluctant to hand over the oar. Behind you, the next rowers settle into their seats, and the strokes go on.
> Watch the darkening sea and sky

You stand at the side and watch the evening come on. The water is dark and immense, lifting the boat and setting it down again in long, unhurried swells. A long rope of weed drifts past, going where the sea goes. Far off, lightning flickers inside the clouds: silent, slow, a light from a storm that has not reached you yet. You search the horizon and find nothing. No sail, no shore, no bird. Only the sea, and the last of the evening, and the wind on your face carrying the smell of coming rain.
> Get some rest

You find a place among the bundles, pull a corner of sail cloth over you, and let the day go. Sleep comes slowly, and against your will, but it comes. You dream of the village, of doors, of a table with bread on it, and the dreams are uneasy, thin at the edges. Still, the body knows what it needs. Hours pass. The oars go on. The rain, when it starts, drums on the tarp above you, patient and small. Then the wind changes. It hardens, the boat leans, and a rope somewhere slaps the mast, again and again. You wake with your heart already standing, and the boat is not the boat it was.
> Go back to sleep

The storm arrives in full darkness, at last, and total. Rain hammers the deck. The waves take the boat and throw it, and the boat, old creature, takes the punishment. People shout. Somewhere a child is crying, and someone is singing to the child, and both sound equally desperate. Then a cry goes up from the front, one word that cuts through everything: "REEF!" The boat grinds over the rocks, and the hull tears open. People and belongings spill into the black water together, and the water does not care which is which. Who do you grab and hold onto?
> Arik

You reach for Arik. He takes your arm with a grip that refuses everything the sea has to offer, and together you go under, and together you come up, mouths full of salt. He coughs, spits, shouts words the storm devours. Then he pulls, and you pull, and by hands and stubbornness you are both moving through the mountains of water toward a dark line that must be shore. It must be. Arik spends a breath on you instead of the shore. "You held," he shouts into the wind. "The rest is just swimming." It is not. But you both swim better for hearing it.
> Continue to the shore

You crawl out of the surf onto wet sand and fall down, breathing hard. The sand holds you, solid and unmoving, the first thing in weeks that has not moved beneath you. The sleep you caught before the storm is spent now, every hour of it, and you let the sand have what is left. Around you, others stumble out of the dark: mothers calling names into the roar, men dragging bundles, an old man on his knees with both hands pressed to the ground. One of the refugees lies moaning on the sand, badly hurt, and the others gather around. Agnar is helped ashore, still half-asleep, awake enough to walk, not to talk. Behind you, the wreck settles on the reef, groaning with every wave, refusing even now to finish. Three things are still within reach, washed against the rocks, and you can bring back only one. The food sacks: the morning's catch, a few small fish, all the food there is. They cannot feed a beach full of people, and there is little reason to believe anyone will share anything with anyone now that the boat is gone; but for the five of you they are the difference between life and death. A bundle of silver coins: all that remains of your family's fortune, and yours alone. Your great-grandfather's axe: your family's heirloom, iron that has outlived three generations and means to outlive a fourth. What you leave, the sea will keep. What do you take?
> An axe

You wade back and pull an axe from the rocks: your great-grandfather's axe, its haft strong, its head old and true. Three generations it has outlived, and it means to outlive a fourth. Your family's name may be all that is left of your family's lands, and this is the shape of that name: iron, worn smooth by honest work. With it you can cut wood, build, and defend what you build. You carry it up the beach. The axe rides light on your shoulder, and the shore comes to meet you.
> Wade back to the wreck a second time

You wade back in, and the wreck is still on the reef, groaning with every wave, breaking a little more each time the sea lifts her. But everything loose is gone: the bundles are gone, the cargo is gone, the cooking pots and the ropes and the last of everything are gone, carried off by waves that owe you nothing. And the last of your family's fortune is gone with them too, silver and cloth and generations, carried off to the deep water.And the food is gone with them too, the whole catch, the days of life the five of you were carrying, given to the water. Only a few deck planks remain, torn loose and jammed among the rocks. Good wood, sound wood, the kind that becomes a floor, or a door, or a wall. The sea does not often hand things back, and it will not hand these back twice.
> Return to shore

The rain goes on, softer now, the storm having spent itself on your arrival. You count the others, and the count comes out: everyone made it ashore. The injured one is being cared for. The wreck groans on the reef behind you, breaking a little more with every wave, but still holding together. There is no going back now, and nowhere else to go. On this beach, tonight, that is not a threat. It is a fact, and facts can be built on. The survivors huddle together on the dark sand around the small fire. Someone feeds it driftwood. Nobody speaks. Agnar sleeps again by the fire. The night deepens. The first day ashore waits for morning.


